     "text": [
      "# Binding Energy Per Nucleon for Iron-56\n",
      "\n",
      "## The Value\n",
      "Iron-56 has a binding energy per nucleon of approximately **8.8 MeV/nucleon**, which is the highest value on the binding energy curve.\n",
      "\n",
      "## Why It's the Peak\n",
      "\n",
      "The binding energy per nucleon peaks at Fe-56 due to competing nuclear forces:\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. **Strong Nuclear Force (attractive)**\n",
      "- Dominates at short ranges\n",
      "- Holds nucleons together\n",
      "- More nucleons → more binding energy initially\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. **Electrostatic Repulsion (repulsive)**\n",
      "- Protons repel each other\n",
      "- Increases with more protons\n",
      "- Becomes significant for heavier nuclei\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3. **The Balance at Fe-56**\n",
      "- **Light nuclei** (A < 56): Can add nucleons and increase binding energy\n",
      "- **Fe-56**: Optimal balance—strong force maximized, repulsion still manageable\n",
      "- **Heavy nuclei** (A > 56): Coulomb repulsion dominates; binding energy per nucleon *decreases*\n",
      "\n",
      "## Significance\n",
      "This is why:\n",
      "- **Fusion** of light nuclei releases energy (moving toward Fe-56)\n",
      "- **Fission** of heavy nuclei releases energy (moving toward Fe-56)\n",
      "- Fe-56 is the most stable nucleus per nucleon\n",
      "\n",
      "Iron-56 represents the \"sweet spot\" where the nucleus is most tightly bound.\n"
     ]
    }
   
